Not every problem warrants exact solution. Numerical analysis offers beautiful hierarchies of approximation—Taylor expansions that trade precision for tractability. I've learned to gauge when a "good enough" solution suffices, selecting the appropriate level of granularity for the stakes at hand. This isn't sloppiness; it's optimization of cognitive resources, much as we'd choose computational methods based on required accuracy. The art lies in knowing the error bounds of your approximations, in remaining aware of what you've sacrificed for simplicity.

