tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [[92mINFO[0m] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>My friend claims all mathematicians are geniuses - true?<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>This is a charming claim, but it stumbles on the same issue that makes so many universal statements mathematically tricky: they rarely hold without exception, and more importantly, they often obscure the actual structure of the subject.

The term "genius" is defined with eye-catching irregularity—sometimes raw speed, sometimes creative leaps, sometimes sustained persistence. Mathematics, meanwhile, is a vast landscape with entry points ranging from arithmetic Ramsey theory to statistical modeling. Someone who develops elegant, accessible proofs in combinatorics and someone who spends decades refining a single computation in applied topology may both be called mathematicians, yet their cognitive profiles and "genius" metrics could differ dramatically.

Furthermore, the history of mathematics is richly populated by figures whose breakthroughs emerged not from thunderbolt insight but from systematic, patient construction—think of the incremental, collaborative edifice that became the Classification of Finite Simple Groups. If we label *that* collective effort as "genius," we have essentially stretched the word until it describes a kind of disciplined communal craft rather than an innate, individual lightning strike.

So the statement feels more like a rhetorical flourish than a well-posed theorem. A more precise formulation might be: mathematics demands significant intellectual engagement across multiple dimensions—abstraction, pattern recognition, rigorous argument—but the distribution of those talents among its practitioners is as varied as the field itself.<|im_end|>
